{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The problem asks 'which of the two properties [idempotent or safe] must an append-only event log actually require of its writers.' SRC_001 answers 'neither' and substitutes commutativity of the consumer's fold—a property that is (a) not one of the two options the question asks to choose between and (b) a consumer/read-side property, not a writer-side property, while the question explicitly scopes the requirement to 'its writers.' Additionally, SRC_001 conflates two mathematically distinct concepts: commutativity (order-independence: f(a,b)=f(b,a)) and idempotency (repetition-independence: f(f(x))=f(x)), calling them 'commutativity (or more precisely, idempotent with respect to duplicate events).' These are not the same property; an operation can be commutative without being idempotent and vice versa. This conflation undermines the core reframe the target proposes.","counterexample":[{"operation":"max(a, b)","properties":"commutative but not idempotent: max(max(x,y), z) ≠ max(x, y, z) in general"},{"operation":"abs(x)","properties":"idempotent but not commutative (unary, no ordering to commute)"}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 defines safety as 'preserving system invariants' and claims append is safe because duplicate entries do not violate the log's invariants (monotonicity, append-only-ness, total ordering). But this renders 'safety' vacuously true for any append to an append-only log by definition: appending to an append-only structure cannot violate append-only-ness, so 'requiring safety' imposes zero actual constraint on writers. The target never addresses this circularity, leaving the claim that the log 'must require safety' substantively empty—safety is not a requirement but a tautology of the data structure. Moreover, the target's own analogy admits that deduplication is 'consumer-enforced' ('HTTP idempotency keys are typically server-enforced, whereas event-log deduplication is typically consumer-enforced'), which means the actual at-least-once survival mechanism lives on the consumer side, not in a writer-provided safety property—undermining the central claim that writers must provide safety.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"A writer appends garbage or malformed events; append preserves monotonicity and append-only-ness, so it is 'safe' by the target's definition, yet the log cannot survive at-least-once replay because consumer-side dedup requires well-formed event IDs the writer failed to provide. Safety (invariant preservation) is satisfied but at-least-once delivery still fails, showing safety alone is insufficient and the real requirement is on consumer-side dedup metadata."}],"target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}